#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069797} :activity/id #uuid "f64241dc-95fe-569e-8afc-913a546e0d46" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048170 :topic/id #uuid "8555552a-fb5f-5455-9150-c473e5580702" :topic/math-academy-id 381} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069719 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068796 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068639 :question/id #uuid "ca60143b-d02c-5497-a134-56444304c2fa" :question/math-academy-id "e-7223"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3979b4ea-910f-5037-b8e8-b0297f31c47c" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069425} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069427} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484264} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499503}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding Cube Roots of Terms Containing Binomials"} :step/id #uuid "20d6dd44-7fc6-52a1-a076-27ddb6da9696" :step/math-academy-id 26723 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069799}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069797 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069942 :tutorial/content "To find the **cube root** of a number $x$, we must ask ourselves, \"what number *cubed* equals $x$?\"\n\nFor example, the cube root of $8$ is $2$ since \n\n$$\n2^3 = 2 \\cdot 2 \\cdot 2 = 8,\n$$\n\nand the cube root of $-8$ is $-2$ since\n\n$$\n(-2)^3 = (-2) \\cdot (-2) \\cdot (-2) = -8.\n$$\n\nIn general, the cube root of $x^3$ is $x.$\n\nLike the square root, the cube root is signified by a radical symbol, but it also has a little $3$ next to it, i.e., $\\sqrt[3\\!\\phantom{p}]{\\phantom{2}}.$ Using this notation, we can express the above statements mathematically:\n\n- \"The cube root of $8$ is $2$\" can be expressed as $\\sqrt[3]{8}=2.$\n\n- \"The cube root of $-8$ is $-2$\" can be expressed as  $\\sqrt[3]{-8}=-2.$\n\n- \"The cube root of $x^3$ is $x$\" can be expressed as  $\\sqrt[3]{x^3}=x.$\n\n**Note:** For square roots, we have $\\sqrt{x^2} = |x|,$ but for cube roots, we have $\\sqrt[3]{x^3} = x.$ No absolute value is needed for cube roots, because\n\n- the cube root of a positive number is a positive number, and\n\n- the cube root of a negative number is a negative number." :tutorial/id #uuid "ad1bc727-9054-5115-83f0-a82aa24dc35e" :tutorial/math-academy-id 1605 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "7e24a880-f9da-5e1e-b0bf-5bb2128d72a1" :step/math-academy-id 4295 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069798}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069798 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068850 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068467 :question/id #uuid "2ca53a08-7b11-552c-983f-96e7a392156c" :question/math-academy-id "e-1357"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "942732a6-ca14-5b61-b8fb-ab9ef81610cf" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049843}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069210} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069418} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484217} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484252} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547039}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cube Root of a Monomial"} :step/id #uuid "eea06921-8121-51a7-86ad-15824cc00550" :step/math-academy-id 4296 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069719}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069799 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068851 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068468 :question/id #uuid "c52719a7-544b-5844-b716-9b9d2999c028" :question/math-academy-id "e-1358"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "11c07788-b01d-5788-b2f1-0a027ea53cb2" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069424} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069426} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484266} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484297} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499461}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cube Root of a Fraction"} :step/id #uuid "f7015e37-4cd2-5ffb-814e-264ca9a0b345" :step/math-academy-id 4299}] :activity/title "The Cube Root of a Perfect Cube With Algebraic Expressions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186068929}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046648} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499613} :activity/id #uuid "71ec2c34-3e2e-5ae6-b69a-b5dbce78416c" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048170 :topic/id #uuid "8555552a-fb5f-5455-9150-c473e5580702" :topic/math-academy-id 381} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499576 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499503 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "205338bd-e2b8-5ef4-946e-60ea49b4acef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt[3]{8-y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498802} {:answer/id #uuid "282d4dba-8d23-5113-9d73-c5f211634733" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2-y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498815} {:answer/id #uuid "49dc74d2-9990-56b4-a55f-41426d3ad2de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1-\\sqrt[3]{y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498902} {:answer/id #uuid "61676d8e-8c9c-5429-931f-fd3bf93c7af5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498951} {:answer/id #uuid "b4a24519-9959-5f47-b1b6-7b079c96d48c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8-y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499132}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499132} :answer-field/id #uuid "69253319-8b21-56a4-a375-b0e2ce7050c3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499366}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "860064d1-6642-51e5-83a1-e8a0a0b4fe77" :question/math-academy-id "q-82144" :question/problem "The expression $\\sqrt[3]{(8-y)^{3}}$ is equivalent to" :question/worked-solution "The expression underneath the radical is already a perfect cube. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt[3]{(8-y)^{3}} & =8-y.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "2966d86a-c92e-5b05-b39e-7b16e7e474c7" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499629}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499613 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069425 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "43bca481-7d34-5259-91cb-874aca82b8b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "9(x-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498885} {:answer/id #uuid "4f0d4325-0f8d-5187-a9a0-6346e4c4c09b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3(x-1)^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498914} {:answer/id #uuid "6831cc29-926d-5a0f-9b44-864c4b8510e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3(1-x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498964} {:answer/id #uuid "75522216-0f2f-585a-a400-b83699659bdb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "27(x-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498991} {:answer/id #uuid "8516910a-3ad3-5900-bf26-9abcee760676" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3(x-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499029}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499029} :answer-field/id #uuid "3c8712f6-aae6-50a7-bb10-c5dc0c11f25f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186067720}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "49c6fb96-74c6-5438-982d-666dbe67b2e9" :question/math-academy-id "q-52493" :question/problem "Simplify $\\sqrt[3]{27(x-1)^{3}}.$" :question/worked-solution "To simplify the expression, we write the term under the radical as a perfect cube, and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt[3]{27(x-1)^{3}} & = \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{3(x-1)\\cdot 3(x-1)\\cdot 3(x-1)} & = \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{{(3(x-1))}^{3}} & = \\\\ 3(x-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "b51e797c-a377-52f5-acf1-019291d3535d" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499576}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499629 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499461 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "34df0886-f589-5a77-91fd-b574dff15d23" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{a}{4{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498850} {:answer/id #uuid "4869d8e8-8306-5d02-89de-d7b839a472df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{a}{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498896} {:answer/id #uuid "669efc65-a593-55a2-9cb2-e9832fd6adb8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{a}{8x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498959} {:answer/id #uuid "9d702685-480a-51e1-930a-6e01b50056fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{a}{4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499064} {:answer/id #uuid "b0037796-de78-55f7-b178-e17d9506d50d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{a}{8x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499117}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186498896} :answer-field/id #uuid "efd8c6c1-d268-5074-8c20-054b800150f6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499424}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "3ee83331-c90f-5e77-b2f4-e2b981936cc1" :question/math-academy-id "q-82157" :question/problem "Find $\\sqrt[3]{-\\frac{{a}^{3}}{8{x}^{3}}}.$" :question/worked-solution "To simplify the expression, we write the term under the radical as a perfect cube, and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt[3]{-\\frac{{a}^{3}}{8{x}^{3}}} & = \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{(-\\frac{a}{2x})\\cdot (-\\frac{a}{2x})\\cdot (-\\frac{a}{2x})} & = \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{{(-\\frac{a}{2x})}^{3}} & = \\\\ -\\frac{a}{2x}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "e5848270-698c-55c8-973b-062f99103d08"}] :activity/title "The Cube Root of a Perfect Cube With Algebraic Expressions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498726}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065035} :activity/id #uuid "291f515d-b5c1-5499-b0e3-d4a9391efec6" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048274 :topic/id #uuid "8ec9e357-9aa8-58e6-a773-83ca66dffe88" :topic/math-academy-id 354} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065035 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065434 :tutorial/content "Sometimes we might be given an equation that contains more than one variable and asked to isolate one of the variables. This means that we need to put the variable we're interested in on one side of the equation (on its own) and everything else on the other.\n\nFor example, suppose we want to isolate $n$ in the equation\n\n$$\n2n - k = q + 5k.\n$$\n\nThe equation has three variables, $n,$ $k,$ and $q.$ However, we will treat the variables $k$ and $q$ as constants because we are solving for $n$ only. We can use the addition and multiplication principles to solve for $n\\mathbin{.}$ \n\nFirst, we add $k$ to both sides:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\t2n - k &= q + 5k \\[5pt]\n\t\t2n - k + k &= q + 5k +k \\[5pt]\n\t\t2n &= q + 6k \\\n\t}\n$$\n\n \n\nThen, we divide both sides of the equation by $2\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\t2n &= q + 6k \\[5pt]\n\t\t\\dfrac {2n} {2} &= \\dfrac {q+6k} {2} \\\nn &= \\dfrac {q+6k} {2}\n\t}\n$$\n\n \n\nBecause the equation is now solved for $n$, we say that we've made $n$ **the subject** of the equation." :tutorial/id #uuid "0a129a33-3921-5f0a-9ed5-78dad53f6359" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8667 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "355ed116-0fd6-5fa9-a009-b66e3a852a95" :step/math-academy-id 142775 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065036}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065036 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064193 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186063969 :question/id #uuid "3b855590-1e40-5dee-a518-0211c627281e" :question/math-academy-id "e-13909"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a1a59be3-afee-56bd-a554-037c5ad99f83" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050622}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064567} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064581} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064782} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186489183} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186489233} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186489239}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving Equations When the Variables are Separate"} :step/id #uuid "a9bd575a-61b6-57ce-9011-797e67ddaea7" :step/math-academy-id 142776 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065037}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065037 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064194 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186063970 :question/id #uuid "0fa3b7ba-5b22-5ba4-9898-0bdfee684683" :question/math-academy-id "e-13910"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "202c3d83-dd90-539a-8e22-9689e936e308" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050843}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064468} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064583} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064584} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186489182} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186489192} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186505841}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving Equations When the Variables are Multiplied"} :step/id #uuid "2716f305-9395-50cd-87b2-8bb22c77711a" :step/math-academy-id 142780 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065038}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065038 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064195 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186063971 :question/id #uuid "6cf331f4-6251-53ff-889b-7762f381d087" :question/math-academy-id "e-13914"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a9f58b68-b706-5e6b-b2a4-851517a1db28" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049734}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064568} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064585} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064588} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186489189} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186489276} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186505773} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186505799}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving Equations Requiring Manipulation of Binomials"} :step/id #uuid "4f80d299-127f-5a0a-a3fa-633defd54eec" :step/math-academy-id 142784}] :activity/title "Solving Many-Variable Equations" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186064443}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046648} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186505861} :activity/id #uuid "99eb6a44-d2b9-5096-b3ab-f9426e7c07c4" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048274 :topic/id #uuid "8ec9e357-9aa8-58e6-a773-83ca66dffe88" :topic/math-academy-id 354} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186505855 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186505799 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2f6031fe-dae8-5f79-8354-ae1008ca4d70" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "u=-\\frac{3(q+t)}{t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505187} {:answer/id #uuid "42e1ca41-9357-5447-aecf-19fa174b44b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "u=\\frac{3(2q+3)}{t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505239} {:answer/id #uuid "c6e30156-92fb-5dac-a0b6-4c80f7ce8c61" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "u=\\frac{3(q-t)}{t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505491} {:answer/id #uuid "c8b1a494-b2fa-50bd-90e0-abef56db21bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "u=-\\frac{3(2q+3)}{2t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505494} {:answer/id #uuid "cedd86ac-999f-5768-b398-f390ebb9ae13" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "u=\\frac{3(2q+t)}{t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505502}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186505502} :answer-field/id #uuid "15fd936a-538d-59e8-a7b2-44862239f5b3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505617}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "ad562e0e-d9eb-505d-ab03-28a6d5a05d76" :question/math-academy-id "q-101844" :question/problem "Solve for $u$ where $\\frac{ut}{3}-t=2q.$" :question/worked-solution "We need to isolate $u,$ so we will treat the other variables $t$ and $q$ as constants. We can use the addition and multiplication principles to solve for $u.$\n\nFirst, we add $t$ from both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{ut}{3}-t & =2q \\\\ \\frac{ut}{3}-t+t & =2q+t \\\\ \\frac{ut}{3} & =2q+t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we multiply by $3$ to get rid of the fraction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{ut}{3} & =2q+t \\\\ 3\\cdot \\frac{ut}{3} & =3\\cdot (2q+t) \\\\ ut & =3(2q+t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we divide by $t$ to get $u$ alone:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}ut & =3(2q+t) \\\\ \\frac{ut}{t} & =\\frac{3(2q+t)}{t} \\\\ u & =\\frac{3(2q+t)}{t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** This result assumes that $t\\ne 0.$"} :step/id #uuid "1f219f56-c36c-5367-afab-e9542307a462"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186505861 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186505773 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5a26a004-ab1a-577e-b192-fb3a04ccd597" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "S=N(A-P)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505286} {:answer/id #uuid "68d1cfd7-eb14-56cd-9f35-c1684245f8b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "S=\\frac{P}{N}-A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505318} {:answer/id #uuid "762431aa-c3f3-5af0-a3df-59f4956ff122" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "S=N(P-A)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505345} {:answer/id #uuid "8cc2c16f-5187-567c-80eb-77c0c76039c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "S=\\frac{A-P}{N}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505379} {:answer/id #uuid "94fee1c4-86d3-53c9-9623-7a0244807af1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "S=\\frac{P-A}{N}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505392}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186505392} :answer-field/id #uuid "170a3946-01f0-5d6d-bd80-98d776b43bb3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505620}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "5dad91f1-4207-55b4-9100-a67ecfbe26da" :question/math-academy-id "q-58390" :question/problem "The formula for the total number of people in a school is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P=NS+A\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $N$ is the number of classrooms, $S$ is the average number of students per class, and $A$ is the number of school administrators.\n\nFind an expression for $S$ in terms of the other quantities." :question/worked-solution "We need to isolate $S,$ so we will treat the other variables $P,$ $N,$ and $A$ as constants. We can use the addition and multiplication principles to solve for $S.$\n\nFirst, we subtract $A$ from both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & =NS+A \\\\ P-A & =NS+A-A \\\\ P-A & =NS\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we divide by $N$ to get $S$ alone:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P-A & =NS \\\\ \\frac{P-A}{N} & =\\frac{NS}{N} \\\\ \\frac{P-A}{N} & =S\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, $S=\\frac{P-A}{N}.$\n\n**Note:** This result assumes that $N\\ne 0.$"} :step/id #uuid "3bb228bb-dd4a-5d6f-9588-57bc6709f38f" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186505894}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186505894 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186505841 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "00a51d7e-d0b1-5453-b475-2cd413c6954b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "S=3VR" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505104} {:answer/id #uuid "0e65ec95-246e-5666-897b-d58dd56350c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "S=3V-R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505130} {:answer/id #uuid "137d5484-9010-5f94-99ed-72015866a46b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "S=V-3R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505139} {:answer/id #uuid "267f5f7a-7688-5c2e-be56-83d72f0c321f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "S=\\frac{3V}{R}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505172} {:answer/id #uuid "7476b59e-dd65-5cbc-ac38-f9047bb2171d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "S=\\frac{VR}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505339}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186505172} :answer-field/id #uuid "d8a85f31-1f36-5d3a-89fa-6bec10d29927" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505713}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "fa1ff475-5c3a-53b9-87ff-1753b8c81cba" :question/math-academy-id "q-59669" :question/problem "The volume $V$ of a sphere can be written in terms of its radius $R$ and its surface $S$ as\n\n$$\nV=\\frac{SR}{3}.\n$$\n\nWhat is the formula for the surface of the sphere in terms of its volume and radius?" :question/worked-solution "We need to isolate $S,$ so we will treat the other variables $V$ and $R$ as constants. We can use the multiplication principle to solve for $S.$\n\nFirst, we multiply both sides by $3:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =\\frac{SR}{3} \\\\ 3V & =\\frac{3SR}{3} \\\\ 3V & =SR\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we divide both sides by $R:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3V & =SR \\\\ \\frac{3V}{R} & =\\frac{SR}{R} \\\\ \\frac{3V}{R} & =S\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, $S=\\frac{3V}{R}.$\n\n**Note:** This result assumes that $R\\ne 0.$"} :step/id #uuid "dbe8ab04-6544-5207-baf9-2343a5f029dd" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186505855}}] :activity/title "Solving Many-Variable Equations" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505100}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134917} :activity/id #uuid "b257a2d9-a21e-5e7b-8297-daf3ed7e1fae" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048868 :topic/id #uuid "c7dda967-adbc-5d28-94d6-a574e5483086" :topic/math-academy-id 2184} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134917 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135015 :tutorial/content "Suppose that we wish to evaluate the expression $\\sqrt x + 3$ for the value $x=9.$ This type of expression is called a **radical expression** because it contains the radical symbol $\\sqrt{\\phantom 0}.$ Remember that $\\sqrt x$ means \"the positive square root of the number $x.$\" \n\nTo evaluate this expression at the given value, we replace $x$ with $9$ in the given expression and simplify.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{x}+3 & = \\\\ \\sqrt{9}+3 & = \\\\ \\sqrt{{3}^{2}}+3 & = \\\\ 3+3 & = \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Important:** You may have heard somewhere that $\\sqrt{9} = \\pm 3,$ because $(-3)^2=9$ and $(3)^2 = 9.$ However, when we write down $\\sqrt{9},$ we *always* mean \"the positive square root of $9$\".\n\nIf we want the negative square root of $9$, we write $-\\sqrt{9} = -3.$ Sometimes, we want both, in which case we would write $\\pm\\sqrt{9}=\\pm 3.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "4457c20d-88c9-5028-8390-390579fa7229" :tutorial/math-academy-id 1610 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "9851ef2d-c687-52a6-8ea1-ff43bff4d334" :step/math-academy-id 4338 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134918}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134918 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133918 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133524 :question/id #uuid "6488c7bd-4179-5b78-bdfc-92973f9a141f" :question/math-academy-id "e-1375"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "779a527b-c35d-5c94-b783-2e6eb117f00a" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048652}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134186} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134341} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483381}] :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Radical Expression that Simplifies to a Whole Number"} :step/id #uuid "87051a9a-ddb4-5816-97f9-79ce959ac930" :step/math-academy-id 4339 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134919}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134919 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133919 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133525 :question/id #uuid "6c9e9059-c72a-5cd4-94ee-33cabb839cab" :question/math-academy-id "e-1377"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "63a2c63d-2329-5679-b426-eea89828b39a" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049923}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134342} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134343} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483366} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186